I'm a software engineer who spent many years writing C/C++, so I
thought patching nethack (on Windows) would be within my capabilities.
Be warned, I spent about an hour trying to follow various instructions
on how to do it using Microsoft Visual C++ - to be fair I was using a
newer version of visual studio than the instructions were written for.

However, help was at hand. An alternative method is to use the minGW
toolset instead - following the instructions linked below, I compiled
my own source, patched it and compiled again, all with no hassle in
under five minutes.

http://nethack.wikia.com/wiki/Patching

See also the referenced page on Compiling first.

I applied five more patches over the following 15 minutes, which was
all fine except that 2 of the patches were not compatable with each
other out-of-the-box (dump and statuscolors) they both make
modifications to the exact same lines of source code. If this happens
to you, you will recieve a warning during the patching process. I had
to manually edit the source code to try and manually resolve the
patching, which I was able to do, but that would definitely require C
programming skills.



